.TH AENIGMA-UNLOCK-KEY 8 "2026-08-17" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-unlock-key \- remove passphrase encryption from the aenigma service's private key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-unlock-key
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-unlock-key
re-writes the existing
.I aenigma
service private key in place, removing its AES-256 passphrase encryption
via
.BR openssl (1).
It is the inverse of
.BR aenigma-lock-key (8):
where that script wraps the private key in passphrase-based encryption,
this script strips it, leaving the key stored as plaintext PEM on disk.
.PP
The key is written first to a temporary
.I unlocked
file, and only moved over the original private key file once
.BR openssl (1)
has succeeded, so a failed or interrupted operation does not leave the
original private key truncated or corrupted. Ownership and permissions on
the resulting file are then reset to the
.I aenigma
service user, mode 0600.
.PP
The script takes no options or arguments; it always operates on the
service's single, fixed private key path.
.B aenigma-unlock-key
must be run as root, since it changes ownership of the private key file to
the
.I aenigma
service account.
.PP
If the private key is currently passphrase-protected,
.BR openssl (1)
will interactively prompt for that passphrase in order to decrypt it. If
the key is already unencrypted, the command proceeds without any prompt
and simply rewrites an equivalent plaintext file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private-key.pem
The service's RSA private key. Must already exist; this is both the input
key that gets decrypted and, on success, the final output location.
.TP
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private-key-unlocked.pem
Temporary file holding the newly decrypted key while
.BR openssl (1)
runs. Moved over
.I private-key.pem
on success; does not persist afterward.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; the private key was decrypted in place and its ownership/
permissions were set.
.TP
.B 1
The script was not invoked as root, or
.I private-key.pem
does not exist.
.PP
Any failure from
.BR openssl (1)
(for example, an incorrect passphrase) causes the script to abort
immediately, since it runs with
.B set -e
and does not trap or ignore command failures. Because the original key is
only overwritten after
.BR openssl (1)
succeeds, an aborted run leaves the original private key file untouched.
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
After running successfully, this script leaves
.I /var/lib/aenigma/private-key.pem
as an unencrypted private key on disk, protected only by filesystem
ownership and the 0600 permission mode \(em not by any passphrase. Anyone
able to read that file as the
.I aenigma
user or as root can use the key directly, with no further authentication
step. Only run this on a system where storing the key in plaintext is an
accepted and deliberate trade-off (for example, so an unattended service
can load the key without interactive input), and consider re-locking the
key with
.BR aenigma-lock-key (8)
if that trade-off no longer applies.
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
.B aenigma-unlock-key
does not check whether
.I private-key.pem
is already unencrypted before running; in that case the operation is a
harmless no-op that rewrites an equivalent plaintext file.
.IP \(bu 2
.B umask 077
is set for the duration of the script, so the temporary unlocked-key file
is created with owner-only access even before the explicit
.BR chmod (1)
call runs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aenigma-keys (8),
.BR aenigma-lock-key (8),
.BR openssl (1),
.BR umask (2)
